      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what? what are you talking about with the goblin stuff? i just need actual maintenance advice for the tenant. can you just give me a professional list of what to inspect without the jokes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. Here are some professional steps to inspect for the issue:\n\n1. Check the AC unit: Ensure the unit is properly sized for the room and that it's not clogged with debris. Look for any signs of wear and tear or malfunction.\n\n2. Inspect the AC vents: Make sure the vents in the upstairs bedroom are not blocked or partially closed. A blocked vent can restrict airflow and cause the room to stay hot.\n\n3. Thermostat settings: Verify that the thermostat in the upstairs bedroom is set correctly and is functioning properly. Sometimes, a faulty thermostat can cause issues with cooling.\n\n4. Insulation: Check the insulation in the walls, ceiling, and attic of the upstairs bedroom. Poor insulation can lead to heat transfer from the outside, making the room feel hotter.\n\n5. Sunlight exposure: Consider if the room receives more sunlight than the rest of the house. Sunlight can significantly increase the temperature inside the room.\n\n6. Air circulation: Ensure that there is good air circulation in the room. Lack of proper air circulation can cause hot spots.\n\n7. Humidity levels: High humidity levels can make a room feel hotter. Check if there are any sources of moisture in the room, such as a leaking pipe or",
